# Liquid-phase epitaxy of metal organic framework thin films

LIU Bo & FISCHER Roland A.\*
Organometallics and Materials Chemistry, Ruhr-Universität Bochum, D-44870 Bochum, Germany
Metal-organic framework (MOF) thin films are multilayer materials ranging from nanometers to micrometers in thickness, physically or chemically adhesive to a (functionalized) substrate and, in an ideal case, exhibiting low roughness and high homogeneity. Various innovative approaches have been developed for MOF thin film fabrication. Among these advanced materials, surface-attached metal-organic frameworks (SURMOFs) are an important class of MOF films. SURMOFs, fabricated in a step-by-step liquid phase epitaxial (LPE) fashion by alternating deposition of metal and organic linker precursors on a functionalized substrate, for example, thiolate-based self-assembled monolayers (SAMs), have already exhibited their utility in both research and potential applications. SURMOFs combine surface science and the chemistry of MOFs, possessing the following unique advantages that cannot be accessed through other methods: (i) precisely controlling thickness, roughness and homogeneity as well as growth orientation, (ii) studying of MOF growth mechanism, (iii) modifying/tailoring MOFs’ structures during the SURMOF growth and thus creating customizable properties, and (iv) existing in the form of thin film/membrane for direct applications, for example, as sensors. This review discusses the oriented and crystalline SURMOFs fabricated by LPE approach, covering their preparation, growth mechanism, and characterization methodology as well as applications based upon the most newly updated knowledge.
coordination chemistry, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), liquid phase epitaxy, step-by-step approach, surface science, thin film
## 1  Introduction
Since metal-organic coordination polymers were introduced by Robson et al. [1] in 1990, they have attracted much research interest. Subsequently, the concepts of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [2], metal-organic polyhedrons (MOPs) [3], zeolite imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) [4, 5], Material of the Lavoisier Institute (MILs) [6], and porous coordination polymers (PCPs) [7] have been proposed and developed. In general, MOFs are composed of two main components: the organic linkers and the metal coordination units. The linkers act as ‘struts’ that bridge the metal coordination units, which in turn act as ‘joints’ in the resulting MOF architecture. This arrangement will produce porous structures where molecules can pass through the pores with the preservation of the MOF structure. The most remarkable feature of MOFs is their extremely high porosity. MOF-210, with Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) and Langmuir surface areas of 6240 and $10400~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, represents the most porous solid material currently known in the world [8]. A wide range of applications have been developed using MOFs based on their diversity, multiplicity, and exceptional properties, such as gas storage and separation, selective heterogeneous catalysis, selective carbon dioxide capture, and guest dependent luminescence. Moreover, post-synthetic modifications, especially the functionalization of their internal surfaces, widely extend the applications of MOFs. Related tutorial reviews can be found in ref. [9].
Currently, most syntheses, properties and applications research on MOFs concern (micro-) crystalline samples [10], whereas many applications such as smart membranes, catalytic coatings and chemical sensors demand the fabrication of thin films and coatings of defined thickness and high homogeneity as well as tunable chemical functionality. Besides zeolites, organic polymers, metal oxides and activated carbon thin films, much effort has been devoted to the fabrication of metal-organic coordination polymer (namely MOF) thin films because of their structural diversity, multiplicity, and attractive properties. Generally, three strategies are favored for the fabrication of a thin film. Direct coating of a gel on a substrate, such as the spin coating method, is popular for the synthesis of organic polymer thin film [11]. However, this method is not applicable for fabrication of MOF thin film [12] because MOFs are not soluble in any solvent. Another possibility is direct growth/deposition from precursors/mother solution onto the substrate [13]. This approach is widely employed to prepare MOF thin film, typically using a functionalized substrate. Layer-by-layer liquid phase epitaxy (LPE) represents the newest state-ofthe-art technique for preparing MOF thin film. It can be simply described as alternating depositions of inorganic and organic moieties on the surface of a functionalized substrate, typically on a self-assembled monolayer (SAM) bearing functional head groups (Figure 1) [14–16]. This approach is very different from the direct surface MOFs growth (so-called in situ crystallization) because the mother solutions possibly contain stable solvated metal ions or polynuclear metal ion complexes, such as secondary building units (SBUs), or even larger MOF nuclei. Not only does this stepwise approach control the growth orientation and precise thickness, but it also provides a unique opportunity to study MOF growth mechanism. More importantly, it allows modification/tailoring of MOFs’ structures during the MOF growth. For example, it is possible to introduce functional groups on the outmost surface to change the surface chemistry of the film. This strategy is not available for conventional one-pot solvothermal syntheses and other thin film fabrication methods. Last but not least, SURMOFs existing in the form of thin film/membrane can be directly used, for example, as sensors.
An early review published in 2009 described state-ofthe-art techniques in MOF thin film growth through 2008 [15], including MOF thin films fabricated by direct deposition from a solvothermal mother solution and a stepwise LPE approach. In 2011, another review summarized new progress in fabrication and applications of MOF thin films [16]. More recently, surface chemistry of bulk MOF samples and MOF thin films was also reviewed [17]. In this work, we concentrate on “SURMOF” which is defined as a crystalline, oriented, and smooth MOF multilayer prepared by stepwise LPE method on a functionalized substrate. This article covers the preparation, growth mechanism, characterization methodology and applications based on the available literature on the date of completion (July 2011). This contribution not only provides readers a systematic knowledge in the field of SURMOFs but also keeps the audience updated with the latest progress in SURMOFs.
## 2 MOF thin film preparation
Much effort has been devoted to the preparation of MOF thin films as recently reviewed by Shekhah et al. [16]. Table 1 summarizes the most reported preparation methods, together with typical examples and substrates. In this section, LPE-based SURMOF will be emphasized in detail, whereas other preparation methods will be presented in summary. Attention is also paid to the newest publications on free-standing MOF thin films and Langmuir-Blodgett layer-by-layer deposition.
## 2.1  Deposition on substrate
The easiest and most straightforward approach is the deposition of MOF thin film from solvothermal mother solution onto a (functionalized) solid substrate. As with any other crystalline materials, MOF formation always undergoes two steps: nucleation and subsequent growth. The filtrated solvothermal mother solution could contain nuclei for MOF growth, as with SBUs as mentioned above. These nucleation sites will be deposited onto the substrate, and crystal growth will occur at the surface.
Even for a bare substrate (without any functional groups, like silica), crystal sizes increase as deposition process proceeds [29]. However, no crystal is generated when the filtrated mother solution is kept where it is. In other cases, crystallization may occur when substrates are directly immersed into the reactant solution driven by methods such as heating and supersaturating. Substrates, especially functionalized ones, serve as heterogeneous nucleation sites for MOF growth. The basic idea is to use a substrate to induce nucleation and then subsequent MOF growth in a 2D scale, thus fabricating MOF thin films. Other methods listed in Table 1 (e.g., microwave-induced thermal deposition, colloidal deposition method, and evaporation induced crystallization) also follow this principle. Detailed description and discussion can be found in the ref. [16].
## 2.2  Free-standing MOF thin film
Interestingly, Ameloot et al. recently demonstrated the liquidliquid interface synthesis of free-standing MOF thin film through a so-called self-completing growth mechanism in an elegant fashion [28]. By layering an aqueous metal precursor (copper acetate) solution on top of a solution of organic linker (btc) in 1-octanol, free-standing HUKST-1 thin film can be produced (Figure 2). It is noteworthy that applying the same strategy, MOF capsules are prepared via injecting one solution into another. The crystallization occurs at the immiscible liquid-liquid interface, which is substantially distinct from the above mentioned thin film fabrication at the liquid-solid interface. Wöll et al. also introduced an efficient method to prepare free-standing MOF thin film by delaminating SURMOF platelets without changing predefined shapes and thicknesses, utilizing PMMA, poly(methyl methacrylate), solution in chlorobenzene as an adhesive, and then removing PMMA by rinsing with acetone [30].
For applications such as a permeation experiment, free-standing MOF thin films have advantages over supported ones because there is no influence from a substrate. Nevertheless, we have to be aware that these films are very fragile because they consist of tiny MOF particles stacked via weak interaction. It is inconvenient to manipulate in practice. Perhaps one can directly prepare free-standing MOF thin film by mechanically pressing and stamping from powdered MOF samples, without changing their properties.
## 2.3  Langmuir-Blodgett layer-by-layer deposition
The Langmuir-Blodgett (LB) approach has been adopted for MOF monolayer growth by Kitagawa et al. [31]. As illustrated in Figure 3, layer-structured MOF nanofilm (NAFS -1) has been prepared by this LB-LBL method, where NAFS-1 is composed of cobalt-containing porphyrin units (CoTCCP) linked together by binuclear copper paddle-wheel units in a 2D array of CoTCCP-Py-Cu, which can be transferred to a silicon substrate in a layer-by-layer fashion. In this way, the overall thickness of the film can be adjusted by the number of deposited layers. Another layer- structured MOF nanofilm (NAFS-2) was similarly assembled [32]. Apparently, this method is not applicable to 3D structured MOFs. By contrast, by employing SAM-modi- fied Au-substrates, the synthesis of 2D layered SURMOFs of $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b d c)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ and $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ can be directly achieved via using the standard LPE approach with the planes oriented perpendicular to the substrate via weak $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction [33].
## 2.4  Layer-by-layer liquid-phase epitaxy
In this section, we will focus on the LPE method, particularly on SAM functionalized substrates. The preparation, characterization, modification and applications of SAMs have previously been reviewed [34]. Because a fairly large number of the MOF structures reported so far are based on carboxylate coupling units and N-based coordination, the desirability of SAMs bearing functional head groups of -COOH and pyridine on the surface is quite apparent. The general procedure is diagramed in Figure 1. When exposed to a metal precursor solution, functional groups on SAMs will first anchor metal units by coordination bonds, depending on the functional groups on the SAMs. Physically adsorbed metal units are subsequently removed by rinsing with pure solvent. Ideally, a monolayer of metal units will be formed on the surface. Further exposure to an organic linker in solution will lead to the coordinative binding of organic linkers (polydentate ligand) to the prefixed metal units, depending on the coordination geometry of metal ions and functional groups of the organic linkers. After cleaning the surface by rinsing, the upward-facing and unoccupied functional groups serve as new reaction sites for the next cycle of metal precursor deposition. The repeated process finally results in the formation of MOF thin films. In an ideal case, one cycle of deposition (one step of metal precursor followed by one step of organic linker with a washing step between) corresponds to one unit cell growth in the direction perpendicular to the surface. Intrinsically, this approach leads to thin films of low roughness and high homogeneity. Apparently, the thickness of MOF thin film can be precisely tuned by adjusting the number of deposition cycles. Depending on the functional groups on the substrate, the orientation of the resulting MOF thin film can be controlled (see Section 2.4.4). This sequential, stepwise approach also allows one to interpose different MOF films on top of one another. In this way, selective functionalization of MOF multilayers at a certain depth and hybridization of MOF thin films can be achieved with integration of multiple properties into one thin film sample.
Early studies gave well-documented details on how to prepare coordination polymer thin film using a stepwise LPE method, including examples of [Ni-(bipy) $\{\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{CN})_{4}\}]$ (bipy $\it{\Omega}=4{,}4\prime$ -bipyridine) [40], $\mathrm{{[Fe(pz)\{Pt(CN)_{4}\}]}}$ $\mathrm{(pz=pyra\mathrm{-}}$ zine) and its isomorphs [41]. However, there is lack of direct evidence for the formation of crystalline materials; those pioneering work primarily brings us inspiration. The materials prepared according to these early studies are beyond the topic of this review due to their amorphous properties. We now turn our attention to crystalline MOF thin films fabricated by stepwise LPE method, namely SURMOFs as summarized in Table 2. These SURMOFs are classified into three types: HUKST-1, layer-based MOF and hybridized MOF (MOF-on-MOF), according to the structure feature of the target SURMOFs. This review discusses essential properties of SURMOFs arising from the nature of LBL method: preferred orientation, precise thickness, low roughness and high homogeneity, as well as tunable hybridization and functionalization which are inaccessible for other thin film deposition methods.
## 2.4.1 HKUST-1 ([Cu3(btc)2]n)
Wöll et al. [23] demonstrated the first successful crystalline MOF thin film by applying LPE in 2007. A two-  component MOF, HKUST-1 $\mathrm{[Cu}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]_{n}$ [42], was grown on -COOH and -OH terminated SAMs on gold substrate by alternatively applying a metal precursor (copper acetate) and an organic linker (btc) in ethanol solution onto the functionalized surface. Each step was followed by immediate rinsing with ethanol to remove remaining reagents on the surface [14]. Well-crystallized thin films of two preferred growth orientations were determined by out-of-plane X-ray diffraction depending on the functional head group of the SAMs. As shown in Figure 4, -COOH head groups lead to the [100] orientation, whereas -OH head groups induce the preferred growth of HUKST-1 in the [111] orientation. This is not surprising when we closely examine the crystal structure of HUKST-1. The SBU in HUKST-1 is a copper paddle wheel with two $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules occupying axial sites in the [111] plane and four carboxylate groups coordinate in equatorial position in the [100] plane [14]. Therefore, a [111] surface, terminated by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ligands will show the highest stabilization energy upon contact with an OH-terminated surface, whereas [100] oriented growth is favored by a COOH-terminated SAM.
## 2.4.2 Layer-based MOFs
Another representative series of layer-based MOFs of the general formula $[\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{2}\mathbf{P}]_{n}(\mathbf{M};\mathbf{Cu}^{2+},\mathbf{Zn}^{2+}$ ; L: dicarboxylate linker; P: dinitrogen pillar ligand) are well-suited for SURMOF growth via this smart LPE method [22, 25]. As displayed in Figure 5, a typical example, $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(d a b-}$ ${\mathrm{co}})]_{n}$ , consists of 2-D layers of $\mathrm{[Cu(bdc)]}$ which can be connected into 3D network structures by dinitrogen pillar ligand such as dabco [43]. Starting with copper acetate, the water molecule in the axial coordination position can be replaced by nitrogen-based linkers. The acetate groups located in the equatorial position may be substituted by carboxylate-based ligands. In a LEP growth fashion, depending on functional head groups applied on the surface, [100] and [001] orientation can be found on pyridine and -COOH terminated SAMs, respectively (see Section 2.4.4). Remarkably, oriented SURMOFs of $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(+/-)(c a m)_{2}d a b c o]}$ are also prepared by applying the same strategy [36], which represents the first example of homochiral MOF thin film and widely extends the future of MOF thin films (see Section 5.3). Recently, 2D layered MOFs of $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b d c)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ and $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ were transferred onto COOHterminated SAM via the standard LPE approach. The planes are oriented perpendicular to the substrate via weak $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions as described in Section 2.3 [33]. This SURMOF showed a reversible intercalation of an organic dye, DXP $(\mathrm{DXP}~=~N,N.$ -bis(2,6-dimethylphenyl)-3,4,9,10-perylentetracarboxylic di-imide), into the space between adjacent layers.
## 2.4.3 MOF-on-MOF
Taking advantage of lattice matching at two crystal interfaces, it is feasible to synthesize MOF-on-MOF structures by heteroepitaxy. The key to fabrication of hybridized SURMOFs rests upon the smart selection of MOF candidates. Two core-shell bulky hybridized MOFs have been addressed, namely $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ (shell) $\textcircled{a}[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}$ (dabco)] (core) [44] and IRMOF-3(shell) $@$ IRMOF-1(core) or IRMOF-1(shell) $@$ IRMOF-3(core) [45]. A B-A-B hybridized structure of MOF block $([Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{dpndi})]$ (B), $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]$ (A)) [46] was synthesized by faceselective epitaxial growth. This elegant chemistry inspires one to prepare hybridized SURMOF through heteroepitaxial growth taking lattice matching and crystal plane properties into account for desired traits and applications.
The layer-based MOF family $[\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{2}\mathbf{P}]_{n}$ (Figure 5) is well-suited for the fabrication of hybridized SURMOFs (see Section 2.4.2). As shown in Figure 6, one can grow MOF-B on MOF-A keeping identical linkers but changing the metal precursors. It is noteworthy that in this particular case, the fabrication of multilayers of $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ on $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ is feasible; however, the system does not work in reverse. One can also design hybrid thin films by changing linkers and keeping metal precursors the same. Keeping in mind the principle mentioned above, hybridized SURMOFs $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(F_{4}b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]/[C u_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ , $[Z\mathsf{n}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{BME\mathrm{-}b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]/[C u_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ , $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ $/[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]$ , and $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]/[C u_{2}(a b d c)_{2}}$ (dabco)] (Table 2) have been obtained and characterized by XRD and other methods. Apart from these binary systems, more recently we succeeded in obtaining a ternary system and achieved a hybridized C-B-A SURMOF $\mathtt{([C u_{2}(b d c)_{2}}$ (dabc $\mathrm{\Gamma_{0})]/[C u_{2}(a b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]/[C u_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]\}$ [39], integrating functional (amino) groups into middle layers.
## 2.4.4 Orientation
The most remarkable trait of SURMOFs is the controllability of their growth orientations. This selective and oriented growth involves the nature of epitaxial growth together with the properties of the MOF, their functions and surface density. We illustrated how the functional groups regulate orientation of SURMOFs in Figure 7, using $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ as a model on COOH- and OH-terminated SAMs [22]. Upon exposing OH-terminated SAM to the copper acetate solution, the -OH group will occupy one axial site of the $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ paddlewheels of ${\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}$ and determine the orientation of the $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ paddlewheels, an equatorial plane parallel to the surface. Subsequent bdc/dabco deposition results in the coordination of dabco pillars on the other axial site of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ paddlewheels in the [001] direction and coordination of ndc in the equatorial [100] direction. The growth proceeds along this route and leads to an overall [001] orientation. By contrast, terminal -COOH groups will substitute acetate groups from the equatorial plane, and thus anchor $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ paddlewheels with their equatorial plane perpendicular to the surface. Protrusive axial sites and other acetate groups will be coordinated by dabco and bdc, respectively, in the next step of organic linker deposition. Finally a preferred [100] orientation will be obtained. This aforementioned principle is also applicable to orientation control over SURMOF of HKUST-1 as discussed above. The -COOH head groups on SAMs favor [100] orientation growth due to their matching with the equatorial coordination sites of the $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ paddlewheel, whereas -OH groups suit the axial coordination sites in [111] orientation (Figure 4) [14].
However, recent data indicate that even when employing SAMs with the same functional head groups (-COOH), the orientations of HUKST-1 vary with the backbones of the SAMs (mecaptohexadecanoic acid (MHDA) SAM, $4^{\prime}$ -carboxyterphenyl-4-methanethiol (Terphenyl) SAM and 9-carboxy-10-(mercaptomethyl)triptycene (Triptycene) SAM. Indeed, MHDA and Triptycene SAMs favor [100] and [111] orientation, respectively, whereas both [100] and [111] orientation can be supported by Terphenyl SAM [47]. Due to the differences among these backbones, the densities and orientations of -COOH groups on the surface are distinct from one another, and that fact is responsible for the unexpected orientations.
## 2.4.5 Thickness, roughness and homogeneity
Because SURMOFs are grown in a layer-by-layer deposition fashion, ideally, the thickness of SURMOFs is expected to have a resolution of ‘one layer’ and thus low roughness and high homogeneity. The thickness of SURMOFs is proportional to the number of deposition cycles. Continuous and linear mass uptake upon repeated and sequential depositions of copper acetate and a mixture of ndc/dabco in ethanol solution was monitored in situ by quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) for the growth of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ on pyridine terminated SAM (Figure 8, left) [37]. Other evidence for the linear increase in thickness of $\mathrm{[Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}$ (dabco)] can be extracted from XRD data by correlating the XRD line widths (FWHM) with the numbers of deposition cycles (Figure 8, right) by plotting the unit cell number N versus deposition cycles [37]. A linear thickness growth of HKUST-1 on -COOH or -OH terminated substrates as a function of the deposition cycles has also been observed by surface plasma resonance (SPR) (refractive index shift) [23]. These data support the assumption of a linear deposition of SURMOFs on functionalized substrates by stepwise LPE method. Direct thickness and roughness measurements can be made by scanning force microscopy (SFM) and/or atomic force microscopy (AFM). The thickness of a HKUST-1 SURMOF (45 cycles) grown on a patterned $\mathrm{-COOH/\mathrm{-}C F_{3}}.$ - terminated SAMs (fabricated by a microcontact printing $(\upmu\mathrm{CP})$ technique) was determined to be around $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 9), which corresponds to one-half unit-cell growth at each deposition cycle (consisting of one immersion step in each solution of ${\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}$ and btc, respectively) [48]. The periodicity along the [100] direction (the growth direction perpendicular to the surface) of HKUST-1 is measured at $2.634\mathrm{nm}$ ; each deposition cycle (one layer) with a thickness of half this value, $1.317~\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponds to a half of the calculated periodicity along the [100] direction and thus verifies the half unit-cell growth. In situ AFM studies on HUKST-1 growth from solvothermal reaction also suggest an LBL mechanism [49]. It is noteworthy that the linear growth mode is established after 20 cycles. This observation perhaps arises from the fact that the very first cycles of deposition correspond to a nucleation process, and it is in agreement with the measurement of roughness, which indicates that the topmost surface becomes smoother as growth proceeds for thicker SURMOF samples (Figure 9(b)). SURMOFs $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ on -COOH and pyridineterminated SAM exhibit low surface roughness as determined by AFM (Figure 9). Especially for [100] oriented film, the roughness is as low as $2\mathrm{nm}$ (rms) [37].
## 3  Growth mechanisms
The understanding of MOF growth mechanism is essential to helping us design, tune and modify the structure, and thus, the properties of MOFs. A wide range of technologies have been involved in this study such as extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectroscopy, electrospray ionization mass spectroscopic (ESI-MS) [50], time-resolved in situ XRD study, atomic force microscopy (AFM) [51, 52] and in situ time-resolved light scattering (TLS) [53]. Possibly, the most direct evidence for important growth species comes from in situ AFM studies [48, 49] in which the LBL-based growth processes of oriented HKUST-1 crystals on SAM-modified gold substrates were investigated. These studies indicated a layer-by-layer mechanism of a constituent $1.5\ \mathrm{nm\d_{111}}$ crystal-spacing step of HKUST-1 and the influence of the step vertex on the rate of growth. This progress is detailed in the recent reviews [14–17]. In this section, we will complement our understanding of the MOF growth mechanism with other sources.
## 3.1  Templating and seeding of functionalized surface
LBL fabrication is a type of bottom-up approach, in which the “bottom”, i.e., the substrate, plays a key role in the growth process. The functional groups on the substrates determine the orientation of the resulting SURMOFs as discussed in Section 2.4.4. Actually, it appears that the nature of these functional groups is also essential for the success of SURMOF fabrication. Selective SURMOF growth has been observed over a $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ and COOH-terminated SAM patterned surface: COOH-terminated zones allowed HKUST-1 growth by LBL approach, whereas no deposition was observed on the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ -terminated area [23, 48]. Nevertheless, HUKST-1 thin film was prepared on a $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ -terminated surface by direct deposition from a solvothermal mother solution, even without any preferred orientation [20]. This sharp contrast demonstrates the significant difference between in situ deposition from a mother solution and an LBL approach. In the former, substrates, even bare, can induce nucleation and growth of MOFs. They can, therefore, serve as heterogeneous nucleation sites regardless of the functionalization of their surface. When the substrate is functionalized with -COOH or -OH groups, SBUs and growth elements present in solution interact with the surface as described in Section 2.4.4 and lead to oriented growth. However, no such “selection” happens on a $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ -terminated surface due to the “inertness” of - $\mathrm{.CH}_{3}$ groups. As a consequence, isotropic growth occurs. In an LBL growth mode, functional groups serve as templates and seeds for SURMOF growth. Functional groups such as -COOH and –OH will first fix to the metal precursor, copper acetate in the case of HUKST-1, in equatorial and axial positions of the $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ paddlewheel, respectively. Physically adsorbed copper acetate deposition will be completely washed away during the rinsing step because only weak interactions occur between copper acetate and the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ -terminated SAMs. The same mechanism applies for the btc linker. Therefore, it is not surprising that no deposition is observed on a $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ -functionalized surface. This comparison offers us additional support for the template and seeding effects of applied substrates.
We also realized that highly oriented and continuously seeding layers of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ on bare oxide and nitride substrates, like ${\bf A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Ta}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}$ , can be fabricated by LBL approach [54]. At first glance, this result seems in conflict with the above principle because the substrates are not functionalized. However, if we take into account the presence of -OH groups at the oxide substrate surface, we can understand the fact that bare oxide surfaces afford the oriented growth of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ by LBL approach. Indeed, it is well known that oxide surfaces are often fully or partly covered with -OH groups, which are generated by the interaction of hydrogen with surface oxygen ions or by the interaction of water with metal ions at the surface [55]. Therefore, the resulting [001] orientation is consistent with the principle proposed in Section 2.4.4 due to the same function of -OH with pyridine groups.
## 3.2 Metal precursors
The choice of metal precursor is also a substantial factor influencing SURMOF fabrication. Shekhah et al. [14] addressed the importance of so-called secondary building units (SBUs) with regard to the SURMOF growth process via in situ SPR monitoring the LPE growth of HKUST-1 on SAMs. The reactants are supplied separately in a layer-by-layer growth mode allowing for the study of the role of a metal precursor in the process. As displayed in Figure 10, applying copper acetate led to successful deposition of HKUST-1 onto COOH-terminated SAMs. However, copper nitrate did not favor the SURMOF growth. It was already known that both copper acetate and copper nitrate are applicable for HUKST-1 solvothermal reactions [56]. The difference between the two metal precursors lies in the nature of species present in solution: ethanolic copper acetate solutions contain dimeric paddle wheel acetate complex resembling the ones present in the MOF, whereas copper nitrate solutions contain only solvated copper ions. The nature of SURMOF growth can be classified as a ‘substitution’ reaction rather than coordination reaction. The former refers to the replacement of a functional group by another group within a particular chemical compound. The latter typically implies metal-ion bonding. In the case of copper acetate, the acetate ligands can be replaced by the btc organic linker, which contains the same carboxylate functional groups. By contrast, the surface-exposed -COOH groups cannot capture the solvated $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ ions in the case of copper nitrate. If we assume that $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ ions can coordinate with -COOH groups, the process releases $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ from -COOH and forms ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ in solution. Apparently, this is not a thermodynamically favorable process.
## 4  Characterization and methodology
In this section, a general methodology and some typical characterization methods for studying SURMOF will be briefly summarized. SURMOFs are defined as crystalline thin films; however, SURMOF samples are not applicable for direct structure determination, for example, by single crystal X-ray diffraction. For phase identification of prepared SURMOFs, one must determine the corresponding reference sample, typically crystalline powder or single crystal with a well-resolved structure. One can then compare the XRD data to identify the SURMOF. To design new SURMOF samples, one has to consider the general SURMOF growth mechanism and carefully select “parent” MOFs, while taking into account desired properties and applications. As discussed in Section 3.2, metal precursors play a key role in an LBL process. Metal precursors containing pre-formed SBUs are good candidates so that “substitution chemistry” comes into effect instead of “coordination chemistry”. Alternatively, functional groups on the substrate are essential for SURMOF growth, especially when looking toward orientation. In addition to the deposition sequence and period for each step, solvents and temperature are important factors that affect SURMOF growth.
SPR and/or QCM experiments give the primary indications of SURMOF growth. Adsorption of metal and organic linker precursors onto the surface are monitored in situ, and the experiments help us to decide the deposition time for each step. As discussed above, SPR can also provide us with information about the thickness of the SURMOF. As QCM is sensitive at nearly the nanogram scale, it is particularly useful to study SURMOF loading with guest molecules. However, one should be aware that the growth may also result in an amorphous thin film, even with continuous and linear growth monitoring with QCM and/or SPR. For a given SURMOF with enough thickness (typically more than 20 cycles), XRD is one of the most powerful ways to determine the structure. Out-of-plane XRD is a useful and powerful tool for determining crystalline SURMOF orientation on the surface because it is sensitive to the lattice planes parallel to the surface. By contrast, in-plane scan XRD will measure the diffraction of crystal lattice planes perpendicular to the surface. This method is particularly interesting because these planes are inaccessible by other techniques. In addition to basic characterization such as IRRAS, UV-vis, and XPS spectroscopy, other surface technologies are specific tools for SURMOF study, although they are seldom used in the field of MOFs. AFM and SFM are particularly useful to study the tomography, thickness, and roughness of the SURMOF samples and to present direct images for easy understanding. Herein, we do not attempt to give a full list of applicable instruments; instead, we hope to present the basic idea for SURMOF design, preparation and characterization. For particular properties and applications, specific instrumentation will be required.
## 5  Applications
Generally, all the applications developed for MOF thin films, regardless of preparation methods, are applicable for SURMOFs. However, SURMOFs, fabricated by an LBL approach, possess some unique advantages not accessible to MOF thin films prepared by other techniques, for example, suppression of interpenetration of the MOF network [35]. The recent review by Shekhah et al. [16] exemplified various applications of MOF thin films fabricated by different approaches. In this section, we will focus on the newest applications of SURMOFs, especially those achieved by our group. These specific applications are associated with inimitable advantages of the sequential and stepwise LBL approach.
## 5.1  External surface functionalization of SURMOFs
External surface functionalization is of great interest in the field of MOF research because it alters surface properties, such as hydrophilicity/hydrophobicity and affinity, but keeps porosity unchanged. Although much effort has been devoted to modifying the pre-synthesized MOF, most of the so-called post-synthetic modifications take place at the inner surface of MOFs. In this way, reduction of porosity is unavoidable. The selective modification of the external surface of MOF crystallites or the interface between different MOF crystallites remains a major challenge. Kitagawa et al. demonstrated the immobilization of functional monolayers on the external surface of the PCP microcrystal by coordination bonding [57]. This is subjected to the properties of crystal faces, e.g., terminal structure and particular coordination equilibrium with the immobilized molecule.
Recently, we achieved the functionalization of a single monolayer at the external SURMOF surface by LBL method [58]. The key to ensuring this layer-selective functionalization lies in switching the terminal ndc and dabco groups to acetate groups in $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ ([001] orientation) by using $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{Ac})_{2}$ as a commutator (Figure 11). Surface anchored acetate groups are located in all crystal faces, and thus, subsequent substitution by $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -bdc leads to single monolayer amino-functionalization in both [001] and [100] crystal planes. By contrast, coordinative immobilization of a dye monolayer in the [100] direction can only be made by substituting ndc (not dabco) with dye molecules containing the same carboxyl group. Moreover, formation of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -bdc monolayer does not require lattice-matching with pre-formed $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ , which differs from the concept of core-shell PCP heterocrystals [44].
Furthermore, the surface attached amino groups allow fluorescence labeling by fluoresceinisothiocyanate (FITC) based on the mild reaction between amino and isothiocyanate groups. The FITC labeling on the amino-functionalized SURMOF $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ was monitored in situ by QCM. A permanent mass uptake was observed. By contrast, FITC ethanol solution flowing over blank and bdc-modified SURMOF $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ did not lead to adsorption at the surface. The control experiments demonstrated that the introduction of the amino groups is the only factor responsible for the adsorption of FITC. The size of the FITC molecules is larger than the free pore size; therefore, adsorption can occur only on the external surface. Because FITC is a highly fluorescent molecule, the surface-labeled FITC exhibits a dramatic change in the fluorescence intensity of the SURMOF sample as shown by fluorescence microscopy. Side-by-side comparison of fluorescence microscopy images suggests noticeable fluorescence intensity enhancement before and after FITC labeling, which is consistent with the QCM data. The samples which displayed FITC adsorption in QCM curves showed higher fluorescence intensity in comparison to blank and amino-functionalized SURMOF $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ ; although it is difficult to estimate quantitatively the FITC adsorption. Moreover, fluorescence intensity increase is not observed for FITC treated blank samples.
A wide range of applications for this SURMOF-based concept for investigation and development of MOF surface and interface, in general, were proposed, including decorating pre-formed multilayers with various functional groups and creating buried functionality inside the film, anchoring hydrophobic groups to protect SURMOFs from moisture, introducing molecular gates at the external surface, and tailoring post-synthetic modifications of interfacial layers by diffusion of small reactive molecules.
## 5.2  Hybridized SURMOF and specified modification
It is of great research interest to integrate multifunction into one objective. Indeed, attempts to increase the number of functions in crystalline solids generally lead to either mixed phases, rather than a single phase of mixed units, or amorphous materials. In the field of MOFs, Yaghi et al. recently solved this problem and reported a large number of single phase crystalline materials, each of which has several functionalities obtained by combining the inherent rigidity of MOFs and the functional flexibility of polymers [59]. The hybridization of MOF thin films has also been achieved by depositing MOF-B on MOF-A, for example, $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{F}_{4}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c})_{2}.}\end{array}$ - (dabco)] $\lvert/\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ , $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(B M E\mathrm{-}b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]/}$ $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ and $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]/[C u_{2}(n d c)_{2}}\cdot$ (dabco)], based on the lattice match between MOFs A and B (see Section 2.4.3). By adapting this strategy, we introduced functional groups $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ into this type of hybridized SURMOF. We grew multilayers of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(a b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ on top of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ [39]. The growth process was monitored by QCM and characterized by XRD, indicating a linear growth involving both $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ and $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(a b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ deposition. In this way, we integrated the inert and active MOF films into one single MOF thin film. This SURMOF exhibits coexistence of physical and chemical adsorption of FPI $\mathrm{{FPI}}=4$ -fluorophenyl isothiocyanate) into bottom (inert layers of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)])}$ and top multilayers (active layers of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(a b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)])}$ respectively (Figure 12). More interestingly, when a vacuum treatment was applied, the physically loaded FPI was completely removed, whereas FPI in active layers was locked in, due to the formation of robust chemical bonds between amino and isothiocyanate groups. Finally, the selective and specified modification of top multilayers in the film bulk was achieved, and the process verified by IR spectroscopy.
Inspired by the above-mentioned success, additional effort was taken to add other multilayers of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ onto the top of $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu_{2}(a b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]/[C u_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)],}$ leading to a more complicated hybridized SURMOF $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco)]/[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{abdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco)]/[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco)]}}},$ and creating a buried functionality between multilayers [39]. Importantly, this structure allows one to probe active groups $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ at certain distances in the volume by FPI. This elegant design demonstrates the power of the LBL approach and may lead to specific applications.
## 5.3 Enantioselective adsorption on homochiral SURMOF
More recently, our group has fabricated the first example of homochiral SURMOFs $\scriptstyle[\sum\mathbf{n}_{2}(+/-)\mathbf{cam}_{2}\mathbf{dabco}]_{n}$ (cam $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ camphoric acid, dabco $=1{,}4$ -diazabicyclo (2.2.2)octane)) [36]. Tetragonal $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{cam}_{2}\mathrm{dabco}]_{n}$ is a typical layer-based MOF containing a well-known binuclear “paddle wheel” zinc carboxylate unit of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}]$ with distorted octahedral geometry, in which chiral $\cdot+$ or −) camphorate ligand bridges these zinc dimer units into infinite planar $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{camph}_{2}]$ layers. The linear N-donor ligands dabco occupy the axial sites of the zinc dimers, perpendicularly to the $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}+c a m p h_{2}]}$ layers, leading to a scaffold-like 3D metalorganic structure [60]. The framework contains two main orientations, [110] and [001] for LBL growth on -COOH and pyridine-terminated SAMs, respectively. The same fabrication procedures for SURMOF $[\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{2}\mathbf{P}]_{n}$ were adopted for the growth of SURMOF $\scriptstyle[\sum\mathrm{n}_{2}(+/-)\mathrm{cam}_{2}\mathrm{dabco}]_{n}$ . As examined by surface $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction in an out-of-plane mode, $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{+}\mathrm{cam}_{2}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{a}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{co}]_{n}$ was obtained in [110] and [001] orientation on -COOH and pyridine-terminated SAMs, respectively. In-plane XRD patterns for oriented SURMOF $[Z{\tt n}_{2}\mathrm{+cam}_{2}\mathrm{dabco}]$ perfectly match that of the simulated one from single crystal XRD data. Similarly, we prepared the enantiomeric [110] oriented $[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{cam}_{2}\mathbf{dabco}]_{n}$ on MHDA SAM.
The SURMOFs, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{+cam}_{2}\mathrm{dabco}]_{n}$ and $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{cam}_{2}\mathrm{dab}.$ $\operatorname{co}]_{n}$ in [110] orientation, were selected as model systems to study the adsorption of $R\mathrm{.}$ - and S-HDO from the gas phase (vapor) in a flowing mode using nitrogen gas as the carrier $(R\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HDO}=(2R,5R)\mathrm{-}2,5$ -hexanediol and ${\cal S}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{HDO}=(2{\cal S},5{\cal S}).$ - 2,5-hexanediol). The adsorption process was monitored by QCM (Figure 13). The results indicate that $[Z\mathbf{n}_{2}+$ $\mathtt{c a m}_{2}\mathrm{dabcol}_{n}$ displays a roughly 1.5-fold preference for the adsorption of $R$ -HDO over S-HDO, whereas $[Z\mathsf{n}_{2}.$ $\mathtt{c a m}_{2}\mathrm{dabcol}_{n}$ exhibits the opposite selectivity, a 1.5-fold adsorption of $S$ -HDO over $R$ -HDO. By contrast, the enantioselectivity of HDO by an amino acid-based chiral MOF $([\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{l}\mathrm{-asp})_{2}(\mathrm{bipy})]$ in bulk phase; asp: aspartic acid; bipy: 4,4'-bipyridine) is very low as reported by Rosseinsky et al. [61]. This very significant difference is attributed to the different interaction of $R$ - and S-HDO with frameworks containing plus $(+)$ and minus $(-)$ forms of camphorate. Experimentally, we can conclude with certain that the homochiral SURMOF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(+/-)\mathrm{cam}_{2}\mathrm{dabco}]_{n}$ behaves as a chiral thin film to separate $R\mathrm{.}$ - and $S$ -HDO based on both adsorption rate and saturation amount of each enantiomer. The homochiral SURMOF of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(+/-)\mathrm{cam}_{2}\mathrm{dabco}]_{n}$ fabricated on QCM substrate behaves as an excellent device to monitor and characterize adsorption of enantiomers in order to gain information for chiral separation.
## 6 Conclusion and perspectives
SURMOFs, as a type of interdisciplinary material, stand at the interface between surface science and MOF chemistry. Rapid development of bulk MOFs provides abundant sources for fabrication of SURMOFs, and most applications arise from the substantial properties of bulk MOFs. However, thanks to the combined advantage of surface science and the LBL method, some unique applications can be predicted for SURMOFs. In this review we have tried to provide a comprehensive understanding covering all aspects in the field of SURMOFs, from fundamentals to applications. Although SURMOFs exhibit high potentials and practical applications in many fields, we need to realize that only a few SURMOF examples have been prepared in comparison with the large and rapidly developed bulk MOF family. To fully explore the potential of LBL-based SURMOFs, the first task is to transfer more bulk MOFs onto the surface and/or develop new phases of SURMOF samples independently based on prior experience. More in-depth understanding of the growth mechanism for (SUR)MOFs is strongly desired. This may give us more incentive to control fabrication on the surface. Finally, we note the fact that no orientation-based applications have been explored, though oriented growth is one of the most prominent features of SURMOFs; therefore, efforts are encouraged in this direction.
